<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aybak</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/1250">1250</a>–<a href="/years/1257">1257</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Aybak, or Izz al-Din Aybak, was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> commander who became the first sultan of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk-sultanate">Mamluk Sultanate</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in 1250. He married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shajar-al-durr">Shajar al-Durr</a>, the widow of the last effective Ayyubid sultan, to legitimise his rule. He was murdered in 1257 amid power struggles among the mamluk factions.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aybak.jpg" alt="Aybak" width="320" height="329" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: American Numismatic Society · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Silver_dirham_of_Aybak.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Izz al-Din Aybak, the first of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> sultans of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in the Turkic Bahri line, was an emir of Turkic origin who served in the court of the Ayyubid sultan as-Salih Ayyub with other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmens</a>, and so was known among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahri-mamluks">Bahri Mamluks</a> as Aybak al-Turkmani. His name means moon commander. He rose to emir and worked as a jashnkir, the sultan's taster and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cupbearer">cupbearer</a>, and held the rank of khawanja, the sultan's accountant.</p><p>After as-Salih died during the Frankish invasion of Damietta in 1249 and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turanshah">Turanshah</a> was murdered in 1250, the widow <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shajar-al-durr">Shajar al-Durr</a> seized the throne with the help of her husband's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluks</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubids</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and the Abbasid caliph <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mustasim">al-Musta'sim</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> refused to recognise a sultana, and she appointed Aybak atabeg, commander in chief.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>When the Syrian emirs refused homage and gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> to an-Nasir Yusuf of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shajar-al-durr">Shajar al-Durr</a> married Aybak and abdicated, after ruling for 80 days from 2 May 1250. From the end of July 1250 Aybak was sultan with the royal name al-Malik al-Muizz. To satisfy opponents in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahri-mamluks">Bahri Mamluks</a> also installed al-Ashraf Musa, a six-year-old Ayyubid, as co-sultan, though real power stayed with Aybak. In October 1250 his emir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fari">Faris</a> ad-Din Aktai defeated an-Nasir Yusuf's force at Gaza, and Aybak's army beat him near Al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salihiyya">Salihiyya</a>. Through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>'s mediation Aybak freed the Ayyubid prisoners and gained control of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a>.</p><p>Aktai crushed a rebellion in Upper and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-egypt">Middle Egypt</a> in 1253, but his growing power alarmed Aybak, who in 1254, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qutuz">Qutuz</a> and a few <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluks</a>, had him murdered in the citadel. The Bahriyya, among them Baibars al-Bunduqdari and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qalawun">Qalawun</a> al-Alfi, fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, al-Karak and the Seljuk Sultanate of Rum, and Aybak seized their property and deposed al-Ashraf Musa. In 1257 he decided to marry the daughter of Badr ad-Din Lu'lu', the emir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a>, seeking an ally against the exiled Mamluks. Shajar al-Durr felt betrayed and had him murdered after seven years of rule. He was about 60.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Aybak's 11-year-old son Ali was installed by the Mu'iziyya Mamluks under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qutuz">Qutuz</a> as al-Malik al-Mansur Nour ad-Din Ali. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a> neither liked nor respected Aybak, though historians remembered him as courageous and generous, and he built the Madrasah al-Mu'izzyah in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>. He ruled when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusaders</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-ix-of-france">Louis IX of France</a> waited at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acre">Acre</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hulagu-khan">Hulagu</a> began to raid the eastern Islamic world. Before their deaths he and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shajar-al-durr">Shajar al-Durr</a> firmly established the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk-sultanate">Mamluk Sultanate</a>, which would repulse the Mongols, expel the Crusaders from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-land">Holy Land</a> and remain the most powerful political force in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-east">Middle East</a> until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Aybak” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…India Mu'izz al-Din relied on Turkic slave generals whom he had bought and trained, such as Qutb al-Din <b>Aybak</b> at Delhi and Lahore, Taj al-Din Yildiz at Kirman and Ghazni, and Nasir al-Din Qabacha at Multan and Uch, together with free commanders such as Muhammad Bakhtiyar Khalji in…</blockquote><a href="/empire/ghurid-sultanate">Ghurid Sultanate · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Shajar al-Durr, al-Salih's widow, briefly ruled as sultan before opposition from the Abbasid caliph and Syrian Ayyubids led to her marriage to the commander <b>Aybak</b>, who became sultan.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mamluk-sultanate">Mamluk Sultanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Many women of the ruling elite had been slaves themselves: the consorts of the Bahri sultans began as slave girls, and Shajar al-Durr, the widow of the Ayyubid sultan as-Salih Ayyub, ruled Egypt as sultan in 1250 before her marriage to the amir <b>Aybak</b>.</blockquote><a href="/societies/mamluk-sultanate">Social Hierarchy of the Mamluk Sultanate</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Cairo, in what is now <a href="/countries/egypt">Egypt</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=30.04,31.24&amp;year=1253" aria-label="Cairo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Aybak"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5091 4996h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5091 4996h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=30.04,31.24&amp;year=1253">What was here in 1253? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
